{
  "note": "Shared truth for a message's one-line summary: what a conversation row, a reply strip and a bubble's quote say about a message whose content is not plain text. Vue implements it in utils/messagePreview.ts (previewTextFromMessageContent); the three native kits implement the same rule over their own content models. `kind` is the content type; `fields` are the payload values a case needs, with these conventions: `text` sits at the content root, everything else is the type's own payload (image.description, file.fileName, location.title, linkCard.title, task.title, quote.quotedTextPreview); `count` means that many items in the list the type carries (forward.items, imageGroup.images); `animated` marks a motion image. `expected` is the summary for each locale. `replyExpected` is what a reply strip or a quote shows when the summary is empty — the summary itself stays empty so a conversation row can show nothing, while a reply never shows a blank line. Every platform's test reads this file; the words come from each kit's strings table (`preview.*`), never from an app. An album with no images says only what it is (`preview.imageGroup`), never \"0 of them\"; a forward says how many only when it carries more than one. An `emoji` case's `key` is a pack key, and its expectation is that key's **name in the reader's language** (`emoji-locales.json`, the same file in all four kits): a conversation row reads 一百分 rather than `hundred_points`. An unknown key falls back to the key itself. A `rich_text` case carries the one line the document reads as; a kit with no rich-text body receives it as `FlareGenericContent` under that wire type, like `quote`.",
